\paragraph{Intervals on this panel (descriptive, post-hoc).} The table gives point accuracies with the cell size and an exact permutation $p$, and no interval; a reviewer asked for the intervals, so here they are, computed after the fact and pre-registered nowhere. \textbf{Wilson 95\% intervals} on the out-of-fold correct counts ($n\!=\!50$ per cell), instructed against equalized, in table order and in percent: Llama~3.2~3B [86.5, 98.9] against [34.8, 61.5]; Mistral~7B [89.5, 99.6] against [38.5, 65.2]; Qwen~2.5~7B [40.4, 67.0] against [36.6, 63.4]; Llama~3.1~8B [56.2, 80.9] against [36.6, 63.4]; Qwen~2.5~14B [83.8, 97.9] against [56.2, 80.9]; Qwen~2.5~32B [92.9, 100.0] against [38.5, 65.2]; pooled ($n\!=\!300$) \textbf{[76.9, 85.6] against [48.0, 59.2]}. \textbf{The intervals agree with the pre-registered permutation verdicts cell for cell.} Exactly one instructed interval contains chance, and it is Qwen~2.5~7B's, the target this experiment already reports as never separating its conditions; exactly one equalized interval excludes chance, and it is Qwen~2.5~14B's, the exception EXP-R1b was pre-registered to test and refutes. \textbf{At $n\!=\!50$ a Wilson half-width is about 14\,pp}, so no single equalized cell is individually decisive and the pooled pair is where the evidence sits: those two intervals are 17.7\,pp apart at their nearest edges and do not overlap.
